{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T204451Z-f240bdb8", "ts": "2026-11-18T20:48:37Z", "actor": {"worktree": ".claude/worktrees/household", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "fix/household-mixed-paces", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "c1b0c5917beee23fa757de0d9078d642862b13af", "base_tree": "1ddfc081062dfc6948e4bd68406bc3f054464bcc", "head_tree": "ecd554f859898d7e94529f388833b9b0dbc3f54e", "head_commit": "fd8dc2efdd2fefd0f7cea5e22034c6ddaf9a771f", "dispatch_commit": "fd8dc2efdd2fefd0f7cea5e22034c6ddaf9a771f", "mode": "final (full review, ready for push)", "mode_chosen_by": "fallback-scope-widened", "stage": "inner", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md"], "findings": [], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The neighbour-write ServiceError branch in DisplayService._write_v2 has no test", "recommendation": "Instance. The `except ServiceError` around `self._publish_feed(neighbour, ...)` in the write loop is new: before this change, a neighbour's write ran inside `_rebuild_beside` and that call's guard covered it. The change record says a neighbour 'whose write fails, keeps its feed'. The two failure tests (`test_a_neighbour_that_cannot_be_rebuilt_leaves_the_act_done_and_says_why` and the new slower-neighbour one) both fail in `build_manifest`, so they never reach this branch. A test that makes `_publish_feed` raise ServiceError for the neighbour only would pin it. It should assert that the wall republished stays written, the neighbour's feed is unchanged, a later neighbour is still written, and the warning names the neighbour. Note that an OSError from `write_atomically` is not caught there. It propagates after the wall republished is already written, as it did before this change.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "The plan's 'rebuilds each wall at most once per write' now holds only as 'written once': the wall republished is built twice", "recommendation": "Instance. When `household_pass` puts a wall before the wall republished, `run_household_pass` calls `build_republished` twice: once to fix its first slot, then again with Keep against the walls built before it. This is deliberate and tested (`test_a_slower_neighbour_is_built_against_the_hall_s_first_slot_alone_and_each_wall_is_written_once` asserts builds [hall, study, hall]). The change record restates the invariant as 'each wall is written at most once per write'. The plan's Goals still say 'a pass still rebuilds each wall at most once per write', so the two records state different invariants. The code does keep the #350 sense of the rule: no cascade, and one write per wall. Stating which invariant is meant in one place would stop a later reader treating the double build as a regression.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The plan carries no governed_by although its predecessor recorded three dispositions", "recommendation": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation. `build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md` has no `governed_by:` field. The product has `## Direction` sections, and the #350 plan it continues (`archive/build-plan-household-schedule-lock.md`) recorded dispositions against three norms: re-architecture \u00a7 The manifest is a schedule (no work on two walls at once), player-contract \u00a7 Major 2 (a republish keeps the slot covering now), and architecture \u00a7 Direction (operation logic in the service layer). I checked the diff against each and found no departure. Every wall's slot covering now is kept: the wall republished's own first slot is carried into its second build by Keep and is never lengthened, and a neighbour's slot now is lengthened only by `_lengthened`, staying under two intervals. The feed shape is unchanged, and the logic stays in `schedule.py` and `DisplayService`. Seeding the field with `prawduct-hook jurisdiction` would record that as conforms.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "A neighbour's rebuilt feed comes back through an out-parameter threaded through four functions named for publishing", "recommendation": "Instance. `_Beside.held` is a mutable list on a frozen dataclass. `_write_v2` appends to it instead of writing, and the outer pass reads `beside.held[-1]`. It travels through `_rebuild_beside` -> `_republish` -> `_sync_v2`/`_rebuild_v2` -> `_write_v2`, and those names and first docstring lines (\"Publish the wall's feed from a build\", \"Schedule the wall against every other wall's published feed, and publish it\") are now true only when `beside` is None. A reader of `_sync_v2` can't tell that it sometimes writes nothing. `held[-1]` also implies several appends can happen when only one can. Two simpler options: return the `_Held` (or the Schedule plus what was built) up the chain, since `_rebuild_v2` already returns a Schedule and `_republish` a build; or split a pure 'compose the feed' step from the write, so the pass calls the compose step and writes the result itself. Either removes the side channel and leaves the publish wording true. Not worth a fix commit on its own; carry it into the next change in this area.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Callers derive `movable` themselves from the same works they pass, and `household_pass` re-derives `movable_walls`'s fixed set inline", "recommendation": "Instance. `run_household_pass` and `household_pass` take `movable` as an argument separate from `neighbours` and `works`. Yet both callers (`DisplayService._write_v2` and the test helper `_household_pass`) compute it as `movable_walls(wall_id, neighbours, works)` from the same `works`, so nothing stops a caller passing a `movable` that disagrees with `works`. Separately, `household_pass.free()` repeats `movable_walls`'s 'works no fixed wall holds' comprehension with one exclusion added, and agreement between the two is pinned only by `test_a_neighbour_with_no_room_counts_its_own_works_as_movable_walls_does`. One option: derive `movable` inside `run_household_pass`/`household_pass` from `neighbours` and `works`, and have both measures go through a shared `_fixed(works, excluding)` helper, so there is one owner of the measure.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/schedule.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Test narration still describes the old pass order (the wall republished first, neighbours in id order)", "recommendation": "Class: test prose describing the order of a household pass, which this change replaced. Search the two test files for 'just built', 'id order' and 'then the walls'. Sites found: (1) the section comment above `HOUR` in test_schedule.py (\"a household pass: the wall republished, then the walls sharing a work with it ... the wall is built against only the slot covering now on each neighbour, and each neighbour is then rebuilt ... lined up with the wall just built\"); (2) the docstring of `test_after_a_pass_every_overlap_between_walls_is_a_named_clash` (\"whatever the wall just built did not see\"); (3) the docstring this change added to `test_a_neighbour_with_a_work_to_spare_leaves_the_shared_one_to_the_neighbour_without` in test_major_2_feed.py, which says in the present tense \"The neighbours are rebuilt one after the other, in id order\" and two sentences later says the opposite. The prose ceiling makes this an observation. The remedy is relational: replace each description with a pointer to `schedule.household_pass` / `run_household_pass`, or delete it. Don't reword the narration.", "files": ["arrt/tests/unit/test_schedule.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_major_2_feed.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "`held` means two different things in `_write_v2`, and `build_neighbour` shadows the `beside` parameter", "recommendation": "Instance. In `_write_v2`, the local `held: dict[str, Collection[str]]` is each wall's works, in `movable_walls`'s vocabulary. In the same function, `_Held` and `beside.held` are a neighbour's feed held back from being written. The nested `build_neighbour` also rebinds `beside`, the enclosing method's parameter name, which is None on that path. Renaming the dict (e.g. `works_by_wall`) and the local (e.g. `step`) removes both ambiguities. Cosmetic: fold it into the next touch of this function.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "A neighbour's unexpected build failure now aborts the republished wall's own write, and nothing records that trade", "recommendation": "Before this change `_write_v2` wrote the wall acted on first and only then rebuilt neighbours, so an exception other than ServiceError from a neighbour's build (an OSError reading its feed or theme, a store error, a bug in `build`) surfaced after the curator's own act had landed. Now `run_household_pass` makes every neighbour's build, before and after the republished wall, ahead of `_publish_feed(wall_id, ...)`, and `build_neighbour` catches only ServiceError. Any other failure in any neighbour therefore loses the act the curator asked for. The docstring and the change record give one direction of the new ordering (a failed republish leaves neighbours as they were) but not this one. Instance: decide which behaviour is wanted. Either say in the `_write_v2` docstring that a neighbour's unexpected failure fails the act, or catch more broadly around `build_neighbour`, with a log, so a neighbour falls back to its published slots as a ServiceError already does.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "`held` names two different things in `_write_v2`", "recommendation": "In `_write_v2`, the local `held: dict[str, Collection[str]]` is each wall's works for `movable_walls`/`household_pass`, while `_Held` and `beside.held` are neighbour feeds waiting to be written. The inner `build_neighbour` also rebinds `beside`, which shadows the method's own `beside` parameter. A maintainer following the 'held until written' prose to the local `held` finds a works map. Instance: rename the works map (for example `works_by_wall`) and the inner `beside`. Wording only, so no fix round is owed.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/display.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass over files_oracle: nothing clears a bar", "recommendation": "Covered: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md and .prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md. I checked the change record's claims against the code. That a failed republish leaves every neighbour as it was holds: `build_manifest` only reads, and neighbours are written after `_publish_feed(wall_id)`. Each wall written at most once, a neighbour whose rebuild or write fails keeping its feed, and the later-built wall naming the clash (`_say_clashes`) all hold. The strict xfail for #436 exists (`test_three_walls_a_pass_cannot_keep_apart_though_some_schedule_would`). Its measured table names N for each row, and two rows (case A and the two-wall row, both 0/N) are held by property tests. The non-zero rows (24/200, 77/300) are not held by anything, which the learnings rule 'When you record something as verified, name what was measured...' touches. But they are dated history in a change record, so they mislead no one into action. No edit wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md"], "oid": "O-10"}], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": "household-mixed-paces", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": "1ddfc081062dfc6948e4bd68406bc3f054464bcc", "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", ".prawduct/changes/household-mixed-paces.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-household-mixed-paces.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "span": null, "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": null, "dispatched_at": "2026-11-18T20:44:52Z"}}
